      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "After intracerebroventricular administration, both interleukin-1 alpha and corticotropin-releasing hormone increase nociceptive thresholds evaluated by the hot-plate test in the rat. Pretreatment with 6-hydroxydopamine or prazosin fully prevents the action of both substances. Moreover, the effect of interleukin-1 alpha is completely blocked by the intracerebroventricular administration of the corticotropin-releasing hormone antagonist alpha-helical CRH 9-41. Our results suggest an involvement of CRH and the noradrenergic system in the antinociceptive effect of central interleukin-1 alpha."},
